[
  "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The two Greek yogurt bowls are positioned side-by-side in the center of the frame on an aged, rustic oak wood tabletop, with plenty of negative space around the edges. The camera angle is 3/4 overhead, perfectly capturing the depth of the bowls. A shallow depth of field keeps the primary bowl in sharp focus while the second bowl sits slightly behind it with soft, natural bokeh. Soft, natural light streams in from a window on the left, casting gentle shadows and highlighting the natural sheen of the warm berry compote. The setting features a rumpled linen napkin draped loosely beside the bowls and a few stray toasted oats and sliced almond shards scattered naturally on the wood surface to suggest a casual breakfast preparation. The dish consists of two simple matte ceramic bowls filled with thick, white, nonfat Greek yogurt. A generous swirl of warm, dark-ruby berry compote is spooned onto the surface, creating a beautiful marbled effect as the warm juices bleed into the cool, creamy yogurt. The compote is textured with mashed, softened berries showing deep purple and vibrant red hues. The bowls are finished with a crunchy topping of golden-brown toasted rolled oats and delicate, paper-thin sliced almonds; no whole almonds are present. The presentation is home-style, featuring slight, realistic imperfections like a tiny smear of yogurt on the inner rim and an asymmetric distribution of the oat topping. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. The scene should look like someone just finished cooking—lived-in, warm, and authentic. Includes subtle traces of the process like a few runaway oats on the table. No plastic sheen, no unnaturally smooth surfaces, no perfect symmetry. Warm, golden-hour color temperature. No text, no watermark, no logo, no hands, no faces. Indistinguishable from professional cookbook photography."
]
